lessons.body:
Finalize, Verify and Commit
Attachment Metadata
TaskFlow - Full Stack Developer 2026

Never trust “upload finished” from the browser
The browser can call finalize even if it uploaded nothing or different bytes. NestJS must verify the object
through a privileged Storage API before marking the application row READY.

Verification flow
async complete(id: string, user: AuthenticatedUser) {
const attachment =
await this.attachments.findPendingScoped(id, user.id)

if (!attachment) {
throw new AttachmentNotFoundException(id)
}

const info = await this.storage.info(
attachment.bucket,
attachment.objectPath,
)

if (info.size !== attachment.sizeBytes) {
throw new AttachmentUploadMismatchException()
}

if (normalizeMime(info.contentType) !== attachment.mimeType) {
throw new AttachmentUploadMismatchException()
}

return this.attachments.markReady(id, new Date())
}

What info() provides
Supabase Storage currently exposes an info(path) API that returns object details such as size, content
type and timestamps. It is a useful server-side verification step before committing attachment
metadata.

Limits of metadata verification
Matching size and declared content type still does not prove the file is harmless. For hostile-file
scenarios, add a scanning/quarantine pipeline.

